# 9. REAL-TIME MARKETPLACE

This is one of the most important requirements.

Implement a genuinely shared marketplace using the database and real-time subscriptions.

Example scenario:

1. Seller A registers on their laptop.
2. Seller B registers on another laptop.
3. Buyer A registers on a third laptop.
4. Buyer B registers on a fourth laptop.
5. All four users open the same deployed website URL.
6. Seller A publishes a listing for tomatoes at ₹25 per kilogram.
7. Seller B publishes a listing for onions at ₹30 per kilogram.
8. Both buyers can see these listings without refreshing the page.
9. Buyer A opens the tomato listing and offers ₹28 per kilogram.
10. Buyer B submits an offer of ₹30 per kilogram.
11. Seller A receives a real-time notification about both bids.
12. Seller A compares the offers.
13. Seller A accepts Buyer B's offer.
14. Buyer B immediately receives an offer-accepted notification.
15. The accepted order appears in both users' dashboards.
16. The accepted quantity is reserved or deducted according to the order workflow.
17. Other buyers cannot purchase the same reserved quantity.
18. The listing shows its updated availability.
19. When all available inventory is sold, the listing displays SOLD OUT.
20. Seller A and Buyer B can view the completed transaction in their order histories.

Implement the same workflow for all registered users.

Use Supabase Realtime or an equivalent persistent real-time backend for:
- New product listings.
- Product edits.
- Price updates.
- Quantity changes.
- New bids.
- Accepted and rejected bids.
- Order creation.
- Order status updates.
- Notifications.
- Listing availability changes.

Real-time changes must be reflected on connected devices without requiring a manual browser refresh.

Use appropriate database subscriptions and unsubscribe when components are unmounted.

Handle connection interruptions gracefully and refetch authoritative data when a connection is restored.

Do not simulate real-time behavior with timers, hardcoded updates, or browser-local state.

